//? Title: Ordered Bayer dithering: actual independent pixel decisions
//? Subtitle: Tiled midpoint thresholds, complete-tile tone and finite-image boundaries
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Start with two actual pixel comparisons, then retain the original40-by64
grayscale gradient. Canonical threshold histories and a native binary-raster
plot expose strict ties, spatial pattern and the limited conditions under which
a complete Bayer tile approximates a uniform tone.
)

\toc

> A tiled permutation of sixteen midpoint thresholds

The native4-by4 Bayer ranks are the
rows[0,8,2,10],[12,4,14,6],[3,11,1,9],[15,7,13,5]. Each rank0 through15 appears
once. For row i and column j the actual producer applies

$$ T_{ij}=255\frac{B_{i\bmod4,j\bmod4}+1/2}{16}, \qquad y_{ij}=255\,1[x_{ij}>T_{ij}]. $$
\alttext(The tiled Bayer rank plus one half determines a midpoint intensity threshold; a strictly brighter input produces255, while ties and darker inputs produce0.)

Every decision depends only on the original pixel and its tiled position. There
is no propagated error, random state or neighboring-pixel dependency. This
permits independent scheduling conceptually; the canonical driver is a serial
row-major loop and no parallel timing result is claimed. Output is an integer
grid with two possible values, not a packed one-bit file.

> Two equal inputs, two different actual thresholds

The two inputs127.5 at row0 meet ranks0 and8, whose thresholds are7.96875
and135.46875. Their actual decisions therefore differ. The diagram reads the
executed pixel records; it does not recompute a substitute quantizer.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ordered_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/ordered_dither.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::ordered_dither_trace({{127.5, 127.5}});
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.steps.size(); ++i) {
        auto &s = t.steps[i];
        d.node(std::to_string(i), {double(i) * 9, 0},
               std::format("Actual input {}\nrank {} threshold {}\noutput {}",
                           s.input, s.rank, s.threshold, s.output));
    }
    std::print("| row | column | actual input | actual Bayer rank | actual threshold | actual output |\n| " "---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &s : t.steps)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s.row, s.col, s.input,
                   s.rank, s.threshold, s.output);
    d.save_svg("build/ordered_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| row | column | actual input | actual Bayer rank | actual threshold | actual output |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0 | 127.5 | 0 | 7.96875 | 255 |
| 0 | 1 | 127.5 | 8 | 135.46875 | 0 |
\image(figures/ordered_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual independent threshold decisions for two identical adjacent intensities.)
\alttext(Both inputs are127.5; rank0 turns white while rank8 stays black. The two nodes are independent decisions.)

> Retain the original40-by64 gradient

The reusable native gradient fixture creates intensity255j/63 in every row. The
original three column bands remain[0,21),[21,42),[42,64). Their widths differ,
so each density uses its own actual cell count. The native plotting helper draws
exact black/white cells centered at(column,−row), keeping original row order;
fixed tone limits preserve even an all-black or all-white image.

These are descriptive binary densities, not a perceptual score or an equality
between each individual input and output intensity. No external image dataset is
required for this controlled threshold-method example.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ordered_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/ordered_dither.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/floyd_steinberg.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto input = dm::algorithms::dither_gradient(40, 64);
    auto result = dm::algorithms::ordered_dither_work(input);
    auto p = dm::algorithms::ordered_dither_image_plot(result.output);
    p.title("Original40 by64 gradient: native Bayer decisions").size(1100, 760).save_svg("build/ordered_original.svg");
    std::print("| original band | columns | actual pixels | actual white fraction | input mean | output " "mean | output minus input mean |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::vector<std::string> names{"left", "middle", "right"};
    std::vector<std::size_t> edges{0, 64 / 3, 2 * 64 / 3, 64};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
        auto s =
            dm::algorithms::dither_region_stats(input, result.output, edges[i],
                                                edges[i + 1]);
        std::print("| {} | [{},{}) | {} | {:.12f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n",
                   names[i], edges[i], edges[i + 1], s.pixels,
                   double(s.white_fraction), double(s.input_mean),
                   double(s.output_mean), double(s.mean_error));
    }
}
```
