> What a complete uniform tile guarantees

For uniform grayscale g in[0,255], a complete4-by4 tile contains all sixteen
thresholds exactly once. Its number of white pixels is

$$ k(g)=\max(0,\min(16,\lceil16g/255-1/2\rceil)), \qquad \overline y=255k(g)/16. $$
\alttext(The white count is the clipped ceiling of sixteen times normalized tone minus one half; the tile output mean is255 times that count divided by16.)

The strict comparison sends an exact midpoint tie to the lower white count. Thus
the complete uniform-tile mean differs from g by at most255/32. This is a
quantization statement for complete periods of a constant input. It does not
assert exact tone, a bound for a partial tile, a perceptual optimum, or a
universal advantage over error diffusion. The plot uses actual4-by4 runs at
every integer input0through255, making the staircase visible. The table samples
every32levels plus255 for readability.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ordered_tone.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/ordered_dither.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <numeric>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> tones(256), means;
    std::iota(tones.begin(), tones.end(), 0.);
    std::print("| uniform input | actual white pixels in16 | actual output mean | output minus input |\n| " "---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (double tone : tones) {
        auto r =
            dm::algorithms::ordered_dither_work(std::vector<std::vector<double>>(4, std::vector<double>(4, tone)));
        double mean = double(r.work.output_sum) / r.work.pixels;
        means.push_back(mean);
        if (int(tone) % 32 == 0 || tone == 255)
            std::print("| {} | {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n", tone, r.work.white,
                       mean, mean - tone);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.lines(tones, means, "actual tile mean",
            dm::graphics::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 255}).lines({0., 255.}, {0., 255.},
                                                        dm::graphics::RGB{
                                                        220, 38, 38
                                                    }, 1.5);
    p.title("Complete uniform Bayer tiles: actual reproduced tone").x_label(
        "uniform original intensity").y_label("actual16-cell output mean").x_limits(-5, 260).y_limits(-5, 280).save_svg("build/ordered_tone.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| uniform input | actual white pixels in16 | actual output mean | output minus input |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0 | 0.000000000 | 0.000000000 |
| 32 | 2 | 31.875000000 | -0.125000000 |
| 64 | 4 | 63.750000000 | -0.250000000 |
| 96 | 6 | 95.625000000 | -0.375000000 |
| 128 | 8 | 127.500000000 | -0.500000000 |
| 160 | 10 | 159.375000000 | -0.625000000 |
| 192 | 12 | 191.250000000 | -0.750000000 |
| 224 | 14 | 223.125000000 | -0.875000000 |
| 255 | 16 | 255.000000000 | 0.000000000 |
\image(figures/ordered_tone.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual complete-tile output means compared with the identity line.)
\alttext(The table retains actual white counts and signed mean differences; a two-level raster reproduces only seventeen uniform tile means.)

> Ties, partial tiles and complete validation

A one-pixel crop at127.5 uses rank0 and becomes255: its mean error is127.5, much
larger than the complete-tile bound. A complete4-by4 image of127.5 instead has
eight white cells and mean127.5. These examples explain why a whole-tile
assertion cannot be transferred to an arbitrary crop.

Complete rectangular shape and finite input checks precede quantization. Finite
intensities outside0..255 are accepted without clamping; their threshold
decisions naturally saturate. Empty images and zero-width rows retain their
shapes. Ordinary work is O(rows+pixels), including complete shape validation,
with owned output and constant driver state. The actual-history quota
pre-admits2+3N logical units: dimensions2, original pixelsN, output pixelsN and
fixed actual pixel recordsN. Default1000000 and zero disables it; it is not
bytes or peak-memory usage. No partial trace is returned on failure. No original
image is mutated or retained by reference.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ordered_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/ordered_dither.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    double threshold = dm::algorithms::bayer4_threshold(0, 0);
    std::vector<std::vector<std::vector<double>> > inputs{
        {}, {{threshold}}, {{std::nextafter(threshold, INFINITY)}}, {{127.5}},
        std::vector<std::vector<double>>(4, std::vector<double>(4, 127.5)),
        {{-1., 300.}}
    };
    std::vector<std::string> labels{"empty", "exact tie", "above tie",
                                    "partial tile", "complete tile",
                                    "outside nominal"};
    std::vector<double> whites;
    std::print("| control | actual pixels | actual white cells | actual capture units |\n| --- | ---: | " "---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < inputs.size(); ++i) {
        auto t = dm::algorithms::ordered_dither_trace(inputs[i]);
        whites.push_back(t.result.work.white);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", labels[i], t.result.work.pixels,
                   t.result.work.white, t.captured_entries);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(whites, labels, "actual white cells");
    p.title("Bayer controls: ties and complete versus partial tiles").x_label(
        "explicit finite input").y_label("actual white output count").save_svg(
        "build/ordered_controls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| control | actual pixels | actual white cells | actual capture units |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| empty | 0 | 0 | 2 |
| exact tie | 1 | 0 | 5 |
| above tie | 1 | 1 | 5 |
| partial tile | 1 | 1 | 5 |
| complete tile | 16 | 8 | 50 |
| outside nominal | 2 | 1 | 8 |
\image(figures/ordered_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual midpoint-tie, next-representable, crop, complete-tile and finite-range controls.)
\alttext(An exact threshold tie stays black; the next representable larger input turns white. Full-tile tone reasoning is kept separate from partial-tile behavior.)

> Verification

This ten-report batch passes 50 CTest cases, 25 directly instrumented ASan/UBSan
cases, 174 compiled and executed public examples, 707/707 scoped documentation
definitions and ten representative clangd hovers. Independent Python checks
verify 5662 records with 27916 checks: optimal prefix-code costs, train-only
Titanic category counts and held-out confusion, and all 5001 actual original
Lorenz RK4 steps. Frozen valid-input comparisons cover all ten producers, with
intentional guard, ownership, arithmetic and endpoint corrections stated
separately. Twelve checkout headers and twelve byte-identical installed
development headers compile independently; an installed-header native consumer
links and runs. All 40 complete programs execute, and 40 native figures have
